APX.br = {"country":{"name":"Brazil","tagline":"Italian immigrants' hill-country sparkling wine, tropical vineyards that harvest twice a year, and 'winter wines' made by reversing the seasons.","classification":"Brazilian wine geographical indications are granted by INPI (the national industrial property institute) under Law 9.279 of 1996, with technical support from Embrapa Uva e Vinho. There are two kinds. An *Indicação de Procedência* (IP) protects a place known for a product; a *Denominação de Origem* (DO) requires that the wine's qualities be due essentially to the place, natural and human factors combined. Each has a *caderno de especificações técnicas* with permitted varieties, yields, area of production and processing, and a numbered seal; many IPs require 85% of grapes from the area, and wines are tasted by a regulatory council.\n\nAs of 2025 Brazil had 13 wine GIs: two DOs (Vale dos Vinhedos 2012, Altos de Pinto Bandeira 2022) and IPs for Vale dos Vinhedos (2002, still active), Pinto Bandeira (2010), Altos Montes (2012), Monte Belo (2013), Farroupilha (2015), Campanha Gaúcha (2020) in Rio Grande do Sul, Vales da Uva Goethe (2012) and Vinhos de Altitude de Santa Catarina (2021) in Santa Catarina, Bituruna (2022) in Paraná, Vale do São Francisco (2022) in Pernambuco and Bahia, and Sul de Minas (2025) in Minas Gerais. Further applications, such as Jundiahy in São Paulo, are under examination. Outside the GIs, Brazilian law separates *vinho fino* (from vinifera grapes) and *vinho de mesa* (often from American or hybrid grapes)."},"regions":[{"name":"Serra Gaúcha","slug":"serra-ga-cha","tagline":"Italian hill country and Brazil's sparkling-wine capital.","ids":["br-altos-montes","br-altos-de-pinto-bandeira","br-farroupilha","br-monte-belo","br-pinto-bandeira","br-vale-dos-vinhedos"]},{"name":"Campanha Gaúcha","slug":"campanha-ga-cha","tagline":"Sunny pampa on the Uruguayan border.","ids":["br-campanha-gaucha"]},{"name":"Serra Catarinense","slug":"serra-catarinense","tagline":"Brazil's high-altitude cold-climate frontier.","ids":["br-vinhos-de-altitude-de-santa-catarina"]},{"name":"Vale do São Francisco","slug":"vale-do-s-o-francisco","tagline":"Tropical vineyards with two harvests a year.","ids":["br-vale-do-sao-francisco"]},{"name":"Sul de Minas","slug":"sul-de-minas","tagline":"Winter wines made by double pruning.","ids":["br-sul-de-minas"]},{"name":"Paraná","slug":"paran","tagline":"","ids":["br-bituruna"]},{"name":"Santa Catarina","slug":"santa-catarina","tagline":"","ids":["br-vales-da-uva-goethe"]}],"items":{"br-altos-de-pinto-bandeira":{"name":"Altos de Pinto Bandeira","cat":"DO","region":"Serra Gaúcha","created":"2022","area_ha":6500,"styles":["sparkling"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Riesling Itálico","summary":"Altos de Pinto Bandeira is Brazil's second wine DO and one of the very few appellations anywhere reserved exclusively for traditional-method sparkling wine. Its 65 km² lie mostly in Pinto Bandeira (77%), with slivers of Farroupilha and Bento Gonçalves, on basalt plateaus at 520 to 770 m. Only Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Riesling Itálico are allowed, from vineyards trained on espalier or 'Y' trellises with a yield cap of 12 t/ha, and the whole process (base wine, second fermentation and ageing in bottle) must happen inside the area. Each bottle carries a numbered seal; the first DO wines reached the market in 2023. Producers such as Cave Geisse, founded by the Chilean Mario Geisse in 1979, made Pinto Bandeira's reputation for crisp, toasty, long-aged sparkling wine.","producers":["Cave Geisse","Valmarino","Don Giovanni"],"nerd_note":"The producers' association, Asprovinho, began work on the DO in 2016 and won it in 2022, twelve years after the area's IP.","rslug":"serra-ga-cha"},"br-altos-montes":{"name":"Altos Montes","cat":"IP","region":"Serra Gaúcha","created":"11 Dec 2012","area_ha":17384,"styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"summary":"Altos Montes covers about 174 km² of high country in Flores da Cunha and Nova Pádua, among the highest parts of the Serra Gaúcha (often above 800 m). Its IP (2012) covers still and sparkling wines from varieties including Tannat, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Cabernet Franc. Flores da Cunha, settled by Venetians, is one of Brazil's leading wine-producing municipalities.","producers":["Luiz Argenta"],"rslug":"serra-ga-cha"},"br-bituruna":{"name":"Bituruna","cat":"IP","region":"Paraná","created":"18 Oct 2022","styles":["white"],"summary":"Bituruna, in southern Paraná, was granted an IP in 2022 for its *casca dura* ('hard skin') white wines, made from a pink, thick-skinned local grape long grown by Italian-descended families. The dry, aromatic wines of four wineries (Sanber, Bertoletti, Di Sandi and Dell Mont) were the first certified; Bituruna calls itself Paraná's wine capital.","producers":["Sanber","Bertoletti","Di Sandi","Dell Mont"],"rslug":"paran"},"br-campanha-gaucha":{"name":"Campanha Gaúcha","cat":"IP","region":"Campanha Gaúcha","created":"May 2020","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Tannat, Merlot, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir","summary":"The Campanha Gaúcha is Brazil's second wine region by volume, a sunny, rolling grassland (the pampa) on the Uruguayan and Argentine borders between 29°S and 32°S. Modern vineyards date from the 1970s, when Almadén planted at Santana do Livramento; by 2019 about 1,512 ha supplied 31% of Brazilian fine-wine production. Its 2020 IP covers 14 municipalities and 18 wineries, with up to 36 vinifera varieties allowed. Drier, sunnier conditions and sandy, granite-derived soils give riper reds than the Serra: Cabernet Sauvignon, Tannat and Merlot, plus Chardonnay for whites and sparkling.","producers":["Guatambu","Miolo (Seival and Almadén)","Cordilheira de Sant'Ana","Campos de Cima"],"rslug":"campanha-ga-cha"},"br-farroupilha":{"name":"Farroupilha","cat":"IP","region":"Serra Gaúcha","created":"10 Jul 2015","styles":["sparkling","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Moscato Branco, Moscato Giallo, Moscatel Nazareno, Malvasia de Cândia and other Muscats","summary":"Farroupilha, near Caxias do Sul, is Brazil's Moscato capital, and its 2015 IP is unusual worldwide: it protects only Muscat-based products, including fine white Moscatel, Moscatel espumante (sweet, fragrant sparkling), frisante, licoroso, mistela and Moscatel brandy. The town grows most of Brazil's Moscato Branco, and its sparkling Moscatel, low in alcohol and fresh, is a national favourite.","nerd_note":"The pilgrimage sanctuary of Nossa Senhora de Caravaggio in Farroupilha hosted the IP's founding mass in 2015.","rslug":"serra-ga-cha"},"br-monte-belo":{"name":"Monte Belo","cat":"IP","region":"Serra Gaúcha","created":"1 Oct 2013","area_ha":5609,"styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"summary":"Monte Belo (Monte Belo do Sul), a 56 km² municipality next to the Vale dos Vinhedos, received its IP in 2013 for still and sparkling wines from a network of small family wineries descended from Italian settlers. Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tannat and Chardonnay are prominent.","rslug":"serra-ga-cha"},"br-pinto-bandeira":{"name":"Pinto Bandeira","cat":"IP","region":"Serra Gaúcha","created":"13 Jul 2010","area_ha":7960,"styles":["sparkling","white","red"],"summary":"Pinto Bandeira's IP (2010) covers about 7,960 ha on the high basalt plateau north-east of Bento Gonçalves and allows red, white and sparkling wines from eight red and twelve white vinifera varieties, with at least 85% of grapes from the area and sparkling wine by the traditional method only. It remains in force alongside the stricter sparkling-only DO Altos de Pinto Bandeira.","producers":["Cave Geisse","Valmarino","Don Giovanni"],"rslug":"serra-ga-cha"},"br-sul-de-minas":{"name":"Sul de Minas","cat":"IP","region":"Sul de Minas","created":"Feb 2025","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Sauvignon Blanc, Cabernet Franc, Merlot","summary":"Sul de Minas, granted in February 2025, is Brazil's 13th wine GI and protects 'vinhos de inverno', winter wines. Vines are pruned twice (*dupla poda*): once to grow the plant through the rainy summer and again to set fruit that ripens in the dry, cool winter, with harvest required between 1 June and 21 September. The area covers ten municipalities around Três Corações and Três Pontas (São João da Mata, Cordislândia, São Gonçalo do Sapucaí, Três Corações, Três Pontas, Campos Gerais, Boa Esperança, Bom Sucesso, Ibituruna and Ijaci). Syrah is the star, with Sauvignon Blanc and Cabernet Franc.","nerd_note":"The technique was developed in the 2000s by the Minas Gerais agricultural research company Epamig and spread to São Paulo, Goiás and the Federal District.","rslug":"sul-de-minas"},"br-vale-do-sao-francisco":{"name":"Vale do São Francisco","cat":"IP","region":"Vale do São Francisco","created":"1 Nov 2022","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tempranillo, Touriga Nacional, Chenin Blanc, Moscato Canelli, Muscats","summary":"The world's first tropical wine region with a geographical indication, recognised by INPI on 1 November 2022. It covers five municipalities along the São Francisco river: Lagoa Grande, Petrolina and Santa Maria da Boa Vista in Pernambuco, and Casa Nova and Curaçá in Bahia, at about 9°S in the semi-arid sertão. Irrigation from the river turned caatinga scrub into vineyards from the 1960s. Because the vines never go dormant, growers prune to schedule successive harvests, often two a year. The IP covers fine still wines, sparkling wines and Muscat sparkling wines; fruity Syrah, Tempranillo, Chenin Blanc and Moscato are typical.","producers":["Rio Sol","Miolo (Terranova)"],"nerd_note":"Embrapa's Jorge Tonietto called it the first region of tropical wines in the world to hold a GI with EU-style production standards.","rslug":"vale-do-s-o-francisco"},"br-vale-dos-vinhedos":{"name":"Vale dos Vinhedos","cat":"DO","region":"Serra Gaúcha","created":"IP 19 Nov 2002; DO 25 Sep 2012","styles":["red","white","sparkling","rose"],"grapes":"DO: Merlot (main red), Chardonnay (main white); complementary Cabernet Franc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tannat, Pinot Noir, Riesling Itálico. IP: 12 red and 11 white vinifera varieties.","summary":"Vale dos Vinhedos ('valley of the vineyards') is the cradle of Brazilian fine wine and its first appellation, a valley of about 82 km² of rolling hills shared by Bento Gonçalves, Garibaldi and Monte Belo do Sul in the Serra Gaúcha. Italian families settled here from 1875, and their descendants (Valduga, Miolo, Pizzato, Don Laurindo and others) turned small farms into Brazil's most visited wine valley. In 2002 it received Brazil's first geographical indication of any kind, an IP, and in 2012 the first wine DO.\n\nThe DO is built on two grapes: Merlot, which ripens reliably in the humid climate, for reds, and Chardonnay for whites and sparkling wine, with Cabernet Franc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tannat, Pinot Noir and Riesling Itálico as permitted partners. Reds must age 12 months before release, and every lot is tasted by a regulatory council. About 11 wineries were certified to use the DO seal in 2025, while the older IP remains in force for a wider range of grapes. Expect supple, fresh Merlot with soft tannins and bright acidity, and some of Brazil's best traditional-method sparkling wine.","terroir":"Basalt-derived clays and some sandier soils on hillsides at 400 to 700 m; humid subtropical climate with summer rain.","producers":["Miolo","Casa Valduga","Pizzato","Lidio Carraro","Don Laurindo"],"nerd_note":"Grape prices in the DO rose from about R$2.50 to R$12 per kilo after certification, by the producers' association's account.","rslug":"serra-ga-cha"},"br-vales-da-uva-goethe":{"name":"Vales da Uva Goethe","cat":"IP","region":"Santa Catarina","created":"14 Feb 2012","styles":["white","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Goethe (and Goethe Primo)","summary":"Vales da Uva Goethe, in the valleys around Urussanga in southern Santa Catarina, protects wines from Goethe, a 19th-century American hybrid grape brought by Italian immigrants. The IP (2012) covers dry and off-dry whites, light wines, sparkling wines by traditional and Charmat methods, and licorosos, a rare case of a GI built on a hybrid.","rslug":"santa-catarina"},"br-vinhos-de-altitude-de-santa-catarina":{"name":"Vinhos de Altitude de Santa Catarina","cat":"IP","region":"Serra Catarinense","created":"2021","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Malbec","summary":"This IP covers wines from vineyards at least 900 m above sea level across 29 municipalities of Santa Catarina's high plateau, around São Joaquim, Urubici, Campos Novos and Caçador, about a fifth of the state. Nearly 300 ha of vines produce about a million bottles a year; the rules cap yields at 7,000 litres per hectare and require blind tasting. Cold winters (São Joaquim sees snow), late ripening into April and cool nights give Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and sparkling wines with high acidity.","producers":["Villa Francioni","Quinta da Neve","Suzin"],"rslug":"serra-catarinense"}}};
